**Read:** [Caustic Soda Handbook — OxyChem](../sources/Caustic-Soda-Handbook-OxyChem.lingua#r_1MRFTA19NDBFBQXC69CCRQPAME), Introduction, Principal Uses and actual grade specifications; [Water-treatment chemical supply-chain profiles — EPA](../sources/Chemical-Supply-Chain-Profiles-EPA.lingua#r_0E09Q6T7CC9HNWZB3N84E7K21A), the entire selected chemical profile, including competing uses.

Two materials can share a familiar commercial name yet perform differently in the user's operation. The needed function may involve neutralising capacity, dissolution, electron transfer, nutrient content, a solvent's composition, a catalyst's activity, or the ability to produce a sufficiently clean surface. The useful quantity and unacceptable contaminants depend on that function.

Write a requirement as a relation between supplied state and intended operation. For an aqueous base, distinguish kilograms of solution from kilograms of active base and from equivalents of neutralising capacity. For a solvent, specify whether water, residue, a particular isomer or another impurity changes the process. For a gas, purity without the named contaminants, pressure/flow and delivery continuity is incomplete.

Construct two possible specifications for the same nominal substance used by different industries. Explain each difference by a physical or chemical consequence, citing the actual method or product document. Avoid converting the exercise into an unsupported claim that a lower grade is interchangeable. Use [Industrial chemistry — chemical transformations, useful products and continuing production](../../../../stem/chemistry/industrial-chemistry/Industrial-chemistry.lingua#r_3AKBAFB2ABJ0671QW6ZBQ0B618) for the transformations that make the material and return here to ask how its function is preserved across parties, delays and equipment. A useful requirement makes the next questions testable.

